react-highlight-words is a React component that highlights words within a larger body of text. Give it an array of search terms and a body of text, and it will mark every occurrence of those terms: import Highlighter from "react-highlight-words";

function BasicUsageExample() {
 return (
 <Highlighter
 highlightClassName="highlight"
 searchWords={["and", "or", "the"]}
 autoEscape={true}
 textToHighlight="The dog is chasing the cat. Or perhaps they're just playing?"
 />
 );
} Refer to the examples or the props page for more information, or try it out in the playground. Installation Begin by installing the library from NPM: npm install react-highlight-words TypeScript definitions are included within the published dist folder. Support Here are some ways to support this project: Become a GitHub sponsor Buy me a coffee

Provide an array of searchWords and the textToHighlight. Matches are case insensitive by default. import Highlighter from "react-highlight-words";

function BasicUsageExample() {
 return (
 <Highlighter
 highlightClassName="highlight"
 searchWords={["and", "or", "the"]}
 autoEscape={true}
 textToHighlight="The dog is chasing the cat. Or perhaps they're just playing?"
 />
 );
} The dog is chasing the cat. Or perhaps the y're just playing? Matches are wrapped in a <mark> element. Use the highlightClassName (or highlightStyle) prop to style them: .highlight {
 background-color: yellow;
}

Use the activeIndex prop to mark one of the matches as active (for example, to implement "find next" functionality). The active match is styled with activeClassName and/or activeStyle. import Highlighter from "react-highlight-words";

function ActiveMatchExample({ activeIndex }: { activeIndex: number }) {
 return (
 <Highlighter
 activeClassName="active"
 activeIndex={activeIndex}
 searchWords={["the"]}
 textToHighlight="The dog is chasing the cat. Or perhaps they're just playing in the yard?"
 />
 );
} .active {
 background-color: orange;
} The dog is chasing the cat. Or perhaps the y're just playing in the yard? Previous Next Match 1 of 4

The highlightClassName prop also accepts an object that maps matched text to class names, so different search terms can be styled differently. import Highlighter from "react-highlight-words";

function ClassNamesExample() {
 return (
 <Highlighter
 highlightClassName={{
 alice: "highlight-name",
 bob: "highlight-name",
 paris: "highlight-place"
 }}
 searchWords={["Alice", "Bob", "Paris"]}
 textToHighlight="Alice and Bob met for coffee in Paris."
 />
 );
} .highlight-name {
 background-color: lightblue;
}

.highlight-place {
 background-color: lightgreen;
} Alice and Bob met for coffee in Paris . Keys are matched without regard to case unless the caseSensitive prop is set, in which case they must match the text exactly.

By default, matches are wrapped in a <mark> element, and the rest of the text is wrapped in a <span>. Use the highlightTag and unhighlightTag props to render a different element or a React component instead. Custom highlight components receive children, className, style, and the index of the match (highlightIndex). import Highlighter, { type HighlightTagProps } from "react-highlight-words";

function Highlight({ children, highlightIndex }: HighlightTagProps) {
 return (
 <strong className="highlight" title={`Match #${highlightIndex + 1}`}>
 {children}
 </strong>
 );
}

function CustomTagsExample() {
 return (
 <Highlighter
 highlightTag={Highlight}
 searchWords={["the"]}
 textToHighlight="The dog is chasing the cat."
 unhighlightTag="em"
 />
 );
} The dog is chasing the cat.

The findChunks prop replaces the default matching logic. It receives the search options and returns the start and end index of each range of text to highlight; overlapping ranges are combined automatically. The example below only matches whole words. See the default implementation in highlight-words-core for reference. import Highlighter, {
 type Chunk,
 type FindChunksOptions
} from "react-highlight-words";

// Only match whole words (e.g. "the" should not match "there")
function findWholeWords({
 searchWords,
 textToHighlight
}: FindChunksOptions): Chunk[] {
 const chunks: Chunk[] = [];

 for (const searchWord of searchWords) {
 const source =
 typeof searchWord === "string"
 ? searchWord.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/g, "\\$&")
 : searchWord.source;
 if (!source) {
 continue;
 }

 const regex = new RegExp(`\\b${source}\\b`, "gi");

 let match;
 while ((match = regex.exec(textToHighlight))) {
 chunks.push({ start: match.index, end: regex.lastIndex });
 }
 }

 return chunks;
}

function FindChunksExample() {
 return (
 <Highlighter
 findChunks={findWholeWords}
 searchWords={["the"]}
 textToHighlight="There they go, the other way, over there."
 />
 );
} There they go, the other way, over there.

The sanitize prop processes the search words and the text before they are compared, without changing the text that is displayed. For example, latinize can be used to match text regardless of accents: import latinize from "latinize";
import Highlighter from "react-highlight-words";

function SanitizeExample() {
 return (
 <Highlighter
 sanitize={latinize}
 searchWords={["cafe", "creme"]}
 textToHighlight="Café au lait or crème brûlée?"
 />
 );
} Café au lait or crème brûlée?

